WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.920
The Earth is indeed an extraordinary planet, and not just because of the almost infinite

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.680
variety of life that it supports.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:21.720
Its very fabric, the land itself, is marvellously varied and impressive.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.960
In this programme, we're going on a global journey in search of the greatest natural

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:38.560
wonders in the world.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:44.920
Long ago, the surface of the Earth was born of fire.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:51.560
This was the raw material from which the face of our planet was created.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:57.680
Born over an immense length of time, the Earth's crust was shaped and reshaped by the forces

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.480
of nature.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:06.960
Its rocks have been carved by the powers of the elements, and by that great leveller,

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:13.600
time itself.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.400
What we see around us today is the result of these unrelenting processes of natural

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:33.960
erosion, a dramatic story of continuous change.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:40.400
The world we see now is the result of monumental changes that are barely detectable in our

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:56.160
own brief lives.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:02.680
In our journey around the world, we will visit many natural wonders, some very famous, others

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:03.680
less familiar.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:32.160
We start in North America, a land of countless wonders, both old and new.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:37.040
From the everglades of Florida in the east to the great forests and mountains of the

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:41.560
west, this continent has many rich and varied landscapes.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:46.600
But it's the southwestern part of the United States that is perhaps most distinctively

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:50.840
American.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:58.720
Here are the deserts and canyon lands of the Wild West, justly famous for so many of the

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:05.720
country's most extraordinary natural wonders.

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:34.400
This is one of the hottest spots on Earth.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:38.600
It's also one of the lowest and the driest.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:44.880
Death Valley fully deserves its awesome reputation as one of the most forbidding places on the

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:52.400
planet, but it's also a place of startling beauty.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:59.040
Back during the gold rush of 1849, the American pioneers who traveled overland west towards

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:10.640
California in search of wealth and a new life were met by this unforgiving desert.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:14.520
They gave its shimmering landmarks and very telling names.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:23.040
Starvation Canyon, Dead Man's Pass, Funeral Mountains, Coffin Peak, Hell's Gate.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:28.680
But in fact, only one 49er is known to have died crossing Death Valley.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:33.880
And today, thousands of visitors from all over the world come here to marvel at its

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:40.280
extraordinary landscape.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:46.000
Death Valley is now one of the United States' many national parks, a concept that the Americans

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.120
were the first to devise and put into practice.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:56.000
The very first was Yellowstone, famous for its spectacular geological attractions.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:02.160
Old Faithful, its most popular feature, erupts every 80 minutes or so, 170 feet into the

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:12.480
air.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:17.000
Thousands of gallons of water are superheated under pressure by the geothermal forces that

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:24.080
are still shaping this part of the Rocky Mountains.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:28.720
Here in Yellowstone, there are more geysers, hot springs and fumaroles than in the rest

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:35.880
of the world put together.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:41.040
Winter on this high plateau is especially dramatic, the steam condensing in the subzero

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:50.800
air and the basins bubbling like witches' cauldrons.

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:55.200
This is the Morning Glory Pool.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:00.800
When summer comes, the intense sunlight encourages the growth of colourful algae that, astonishingly,

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:05.520
are able to tolerate the almost boiling water that rises in these springs.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:16.320
The vivid blue comes from minerals dissolved under pressure in its crystal clear water.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:21.400
Long before white men set eyes on this bizarre landscape, the Crow Indians of the Yellowstone

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:29.280
Plateau called it the Land of Vapours and a sacred place of powerful water.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:36.640
As the hot water cools, it creates fantastic sculptures of crystalline limestone.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:40.760
The red and yellow staining of the new white rock comes from the sulphur in the spring

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:52.560
water, giving the name Yellowstone to this curiously beautiful place.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:57.960
Yellowstone lies right on the continental divide, the watershed of North America.

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:05.600
Its rivers flow either east to the Atlantic or west to the Pacific.

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:11.480
The Snake River is one of those that heads westwards from the Rockies, passing just south

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:15.800
of the spectacular Teton Range.

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:42.120
These peaks are the youngest of the Rocky Mountains, not yet worn down by time.

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:49.400
Next of the Rockies lies the real Wild West of America.

00:07:49.400 --> 00:08:09.160
This is Monument Valley, made famous by Hollywood in so many western films.

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:15.200
This desert landscape forms part of the Navajo Nation Tribal Park, which straddles the border

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:21.240
between Utah and Arizona.

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:27.240
The sandstones and other rocks of this plateau were laid down 270 million years ago.

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:36.560
Many have been eroded into extraordinary shapes, vividly coloured by oxides of iron and manganese.

00:08:36.560 --> 00:09:02.320
What we see today is just one stage in an unrelenting process.

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:19.560
Even a few more million years of erosion, they will all have disappeared.

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:24.800
The amphitheatre of Bryce Canyon displays an unrivalled range of the patterns that

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:28.640
wind and rain can create from rock.

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:34.240
This maze of colourful, almost translucent pinnacles called hoodoos has been sculptured

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:40.800
by water and by the effects of heat and cold.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:46.280
This is part of the high Colorado Plateau, often baking hot by day and freezing cold

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:51.120
by night.

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:56.920
The cycle of freeze and thaw cracks the layers of sandstone and limestone, creating these

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:14.440
intricate formations.

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:18.720
The Paiute Indians who once lived on the rim of this canyon believed the hoodoos to be

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:25.520
bad people who, as punishment, had been turned to rock.

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:31.120
When European settlers first discovered this spectacle, they drew on their own mythology

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:34.960
to name these curious formations.

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:44.640
This is Thor's hammer, wielded by the Nordic god of thunder.

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:49.240
Afternoon storms bring more rain which floods down the face of the pinnacles, deepening

00:10:49.240 --> 00:11:06.200
the gullies and reshaping the canyon.

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:11.640
Seen from space, the most conspicuous feature of the desert southwest is the course of

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:20.040
the Colorado River and the Grand Canyon.

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:25.240
In just a few million years, this river has cut through the ancient Colorado Plateau,

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:36.000
creating a canyon 277 miles long and in some places 18 miles wide.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:43.600
At its deepest, the river now flows a mile below the level of the canyon rim.

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:54.440
What is revealed is a giant slice of the earth's history.

00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:59.280
Its most ancient rocks are almost two billion years old, which is nearly half the age of

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:03.560
the earth itself.

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:08.160
The world's largest canyon creates its own awesome weather.

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:17.160
One day can bring both blistering desert sunshine and squalls of snow.

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:26.000
But another more fundamental force is at work.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:31.080
The rim of the Grand Canyon is still rising and consequently the river below is still

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:44.360
cutting its way ever deeper through the rocks.

00:12:44.360 --> 00:12:51.160
In 1883, German settlers in Carlsbad, New Mexico discovered a cave system that turned

00:12:51.160 --> 00:13:03.400
out to be the largest and deepest in North America, a place of extraordinary beauty.

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:10.640
Slightly acidic water seeps through the ceilings of these vast limestone caverns.

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:20.520
As each droplet falls into the cool chamber, it deposits a tiny veneer of limestone.

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:26.960
So the stalagmites and stalactites grow invisibly to us, but very rapidly indeed compared to

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:34.100
most of the processes that shape the earth.

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:39.480
Each year, thousands of visitors venture down to see for themselves these spectacular limestone

00:13:39.480 --> 00:14:02.060
creations.

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:19.740
High in the Sierra Mountains of California lies a valley treasured by many Americans

00:14:19.740 --> 00:14:28.080
and visitors from abroad, Yosemite National Park.

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:33.780
Its famous landmark, Half Dome, was sliced by the power of a glacier which forced its

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:40.600
way down the canyon.

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:51.360
Today's melt waters follow the same route.

00:14:51.360 --> 00:15:00.480
Twenty thousand years ago, ice filled this valley nearly to the top of Half Dome.

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:05.960
In place of those glaciers, giant waterfalls now leap down steep cliffs to reach the valley

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:09.160
floor.

00:15:09.160 --> 00:15:13.960
Yosemite Falls drops nearly two and a half thousand feet, giving it the distinction

00:15:13.960 --> 00:15:37.400
of being the tallest in North America.

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:44.480
We travel south now, across the narrow land bridge that links North and South America.

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:49.280
Two thirds of the South American continent lies within the tropics, giving a special

00:15:49.280 --> 00:16:06.200
character to this vibrant land.

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:11.760
The 15th century Inca settlement of Machu Picchu is one of South America's many ancient

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:12.760
wonders.

00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:36.880
Elsewhere, in the lowlands, humanity has left fuel marks.

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:42.600
South America is the land of immense rivers and vast wildernesses.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:48.160
It's also a continent with many civilisations, some of them ancient and some dazzling new

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:49.160
ones.

00:16:49.160 --> 00:17:04.840
But it's the natural, timeless wonders that we are here to investigate.

00:17:04.840 --> 00:17:09.680
This for me is the way to find the essence of the continent.

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:16.120
Far from the excitements of Rio and Brasilia, drifting down one of the rivers, bounded only

00:17:16.120 --> 00:17:24.560
by the forest and the sky.

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:29.360
It was from the sky that one of the world's greatest natural wonders was discovered by

00:17:29.360 --> 00:17:32.520
the outside world.

00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:39.560
In 1935, flying his single-engine plane high above the Venezuelan jungle, American adventurer

00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:48.200
Jim Angel encountered this cloud-shrouded plateau from which tumble countless waterfalls.

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:56.120
Banking to avoid the flat top of one of these high table mountains, he circled for another

00:17:56.120 --> 00:17:57.680
look.

00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:02.960
To his amazement, he saw below him a slender ribbon of water dropping sheer down to the

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:04.600
savanna far below.

00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:07.680
It fell 15 times the height of Niagara.

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:10.640
It's a leap even greater than Yosemite Falls.

00:18:10.640 --> 00:18:16.320
In fact, it's the highest waterfall in the world, and it's named in honour of its discoverer,

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:24.760
Angel Falls.

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:29.000
South America is a land of water.

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:46.400
In the wet season, its rivers swell to bursting point.

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:51.720
In January and February, the Iguacu Falls on the borders of Brazil and Argentina are

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:55.400
at their most impressive.

00:18:55.400 --> 00:19:01.080
Every second, 10,000 tons of water tumble from the Paraná Plateau and thunder down

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:11.520
into a giant horseshoe canyon.

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:18.080
The panorama is overwhelming, as more than 200 individual falls cascade over the two-and-a-half-mile

00:19:18.080 --> 00:19:31.200
stream.

00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:37.400
But no river surpasses the scale and vital statistics of the Amazon.

00:19:37.400 --> 00:19:44.400
It travels 4,000 miles from the Andes to reach the Atlantic Ocean.

00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:50.400
On its way, it's joined by more than 1,000 tributaries, which together drain a third

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:53.200
of South America.

00:19:53.200 --> 00:20:05.440
Surprisingly, the Amazon basin is nowhere much more than 600 feet above sea level.

00:20:05.440 --> 00:20:10.200
For half its length, the river flows through the world's greatest rainforest, an area the

00:20:10.200 --> 00:20:14.480
size of Western Europe.

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:20.160
Eventually, the tropical vegetation gives way to open water.

00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:33.320
Seven of the Amazon's major tributaries are more than 1,000 miles long.

00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:39.960
South America's other magnificent natural feature extends 5,500 miles along its entire

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:42.160
western rim.

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:47.480
The Andes form the longest chain of mountains in the world.

00:20:47.480 --> 00:21:00.720
Forty of the peaks are taller than any in North America.

00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:07.480
This is Torres del Pino in southern Chile, part of a spectacular massif of granite mountains

00:21:07.480 --> 00:21:12.560
jagged because they're still geologically young.

00:21:12.560 --> 00:21:41.120
Lashed by fierce winds even in summer, their summit's often hidden in cloud.

00:21:41.120 --> 00:21:50.960
The razor-sharp contours of the southern Andean mountains have been carved by successive

00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:58.460
ice ages, and glaciers still gnaw at their foundations.

00:21:58.460 --> 00:22:03.840
In Argentina's Glacier National Park, several great rivers of ice force their way down from

00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:10.800
the Patagonian ice sheet.

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:17.280
Tons of rock debris mark where the glaciers collide and combine their monumental forces.

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:44.680
In summer, the Moreno Glacier, a 22-mile tongue of ice, collapses into a lake.

00:22:44.680 --> 00:23:13.840
This part of South America is one of the few places on Earth where the ice is still advancing.

00:23:13.840 --> 00:23:24.080
The two American continents are bounded to the west by the Pacific, the world's largest

00:23:24.080 --> 00:23:26.520
ocean.

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:31.600
Scattered across its vast expanse are thousands of islands, none more dramatic than those

00:23:31.600 --> 00:23:40.000
of Hawaii.

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:58.880
The primordial forces that created the Earth are nowhere more evident than here.

00:23:58.880 --> 00:24:04.240
Red-hot lava spews from the volcanoes of Big Island, the youngest and largest of the

00:24:04.240 --> 00:24:16.680
Hawaiian chain.

00:24:16.680 --> 00:24:22.680
A hot spot in the Earth's mantle forces magma to the surface, adding year by year to the

00:24:22.680 --> 00:24:36.320
10,000 cubic miles of molten rock that, in just half a million years, has built this

00:24:36.320 --> 00:24:38.200
island.

00:24:38.200 --> 00:24:43.120
From its base on the ocean floor to the space observatories on its highest summit, the Big

00:24:43.120 --> 00:24:50.760
Island of Hawaii rises 30,000 feet, which makes this half-submerged mountain taller

00:24:50.760 --> 00:24:59.240
than Everest.

00:24:59.240 --> 00:25:06.520
Most volcanic islands across the Pacific are much older and much smaller than Big Island.

00:25:06.520 --> 00:25:11.800
Over geological time, they've been eroded by wind and rain until all that remains above

00:25:11.800 --> 00:25:21.660
the surface are the remnants of the rim of the crater.

00:25:21.660 --> 00:25:28.000
As the extinct volcano sinks still further, coral grows, forming an atoll with a central

00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:41.760
lagoon, our idea of a South Sea paradise.

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:48.360
Many atolls of the tropical Pacific barely hold their heads above water.

00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:53.520
If our planet continues to warm and the sea level rises, they may, before long, disappear

00:25:53.520 --> 00:26:10.200
totally beneath the waves.

00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:20.480
Sunrise over Mount Fuji, Japan's tallest mountain.

00:26:20.480 --> 00:26:26.680
The perfectly symmetrical cone of this volcano has not erupted since 1707 and remains a

00:26:26.680 --> 00:26:34.400
much-respected natural wonder of the Orient.

00:26:34.400 --> 00:26:40.400
Asia is the largest continent, and here are many man-made wonders.

00:26:40.400 --> 00:26:46.120
The Great Wall of China dates back more than 2,000 years, during which time China has become

00:26:46.120 --> 00:26:51.680
the most populated nation on Earth.

00:26:51.680 --> 00:27:00.800
But despite the impact of so many people, this diverse country has retained many natural

00:27:00.800 --> 00:27:27.760
wonders, notably this magical place, the Valley of Guilin, 350 miles northwest of Hong Kong.

00:27:27.760 --> 00:27:34.120
This valley is celebrated by the Chinese people as their country's most beautiful and inspiring

00:27:34.120 --> 00:27:43.120
landscape.

00:27:43.120 --> 00:27:48.960
Further west, in India, the majestic Taj Mahal must be counted among mankind's loveliest

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:51.400
creations.

00:27:51.400 --> 00:28:01.320
It represents just one of a mosaic of cultures that fills this crowded quarter of the world.

00:28:01.320 --> 00:28:07.960
Seen from space, Asia's greatest natural wonder is on a different scale.

00:28:07.960 --> 00:28:13.000
The Himalayas started rising 30 million years ago to become the highest mountain range on

00:28:13.000 --> 00:28:17.200
Earth.

00:28:17.200 --> 00:28:22.920
Looking down on the countless Himalayan peaks, Mount Everest is conspicuously the tallest,

00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:31.640
reaching over 29,000 feet.

00:28:31.640 --> 00:28:50.880
There on the roof of the world, the elements of the sky do fearful battle.

00:28:50.880 --> 00:29:20.800
Every continent has its own spectacular mountain

00:29:20.800 --> 00:29:23.960
scenery and Europe is no exception.

00:29:23.960 --> 00:29:28.760
The Alps may not be as high as the Himalayas, but they stretch for 600 miles across half

00:29:28.760 --> 00:29:34.500
a dozen different countries and they create their own marvelously romantic scenery in

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:40.560
which you can find every kind of natural wonder.

00:29:40.560 --> 00:29:52.320
Of the Alps' several hundred peaks, Mont Blanc is the highest, a glistening white dome.

00:29:52.320 --> 00:29:56.560
The pyramid-shaped summit of the Matterhorn has made it one of the best-known mountains

00:29:56.560 --> 00:29:57.560
in the world.

00:29:57.560 --> 00:30:04.000
Dark and austere in summer, brilliant white in winter, it's known as the most noble rock

00:30:04.000 --> 00:30:13.640
of Europe.

00:30:13.640 --> 00:30:19.840
From these peaks, massive glaciers surge down the mountain valleys and it's here that the

00:30:19.840 --> 00:30:34.680
great rivers of Europe have their beginnings, the Rhine, the Rhône and the Danube.

00:30:34.680 --> 00:30:40.600
This mountain village near Salzburg in Austria has a surprise alpine attraction.

00:30:40.600 --> 00:30:48.320
Here, a cable railway brings visitors up to see a natural wonder that was created between

00:30:48.320 --> 00:30:51.760
40 and 60 million years ago.

00:30:51.760 --> 00:30:57.480
Then a great river gushed from a hole in the mountainside, having created a cave system

00:30:57.480 --> 00:31:08.680
that today is known as the Eis Riesenwelt, the world of the ice giants.

00:31:08.680 --> 00:31:12.440
You enter the cave by a narrow doorway which keeps in the cold.

00:31:12.440 --> 00:31:27.480
When it opens, freezing air blasts out, literally taking your breath away.

00:31:27.480 --> 00:31:32.160
Visitors pick their way up a series of boardwalks and 700 steps.

00:31:32.160 --> 00:31:36.480
The steep route follows the course of the ancient river that carved its way through

00:31:36.480 --> 00:31:40.800
the rock.

00:31:40.800 --> 00:31:45.920
Up at this altitude, the air in the cave remains below freezing and any water dripping into

00:31:45.920 --> 00:31:56.760
it freezes into ice formations which slowly change their shapes.

00:31:56.760 --> 00:32:03.000
Unlike stalactites and stalagmites in other caves, these formations are not made of mineral

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:09.840
but of ice and they're constantly growing and changing, especially now in spring when

00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:15.400
the snowfields above are melting and water is seeping through the mountain to form this

00:32:15.400 --> 00:32:44.520
underground wonderland.

00:32:45.400 --> 00:32:52.440
Several of the formations have been given fairy tale names.

00:32:52.440 --> 00:32:57.280
This is Humia's castle.

00:32:57.280 --> 00:33:04.360
Others seem to resemble animals such as this giant jellyfish and, a long way from home,

00:33:04.360 --> 00:33:10.360
a polar bear.

00:33:10.360 --> 00:33:24.520
A thousand miles north, above the Arctic Circle, is some of Europe's wildest scenery.

00:33:24.520 --> 00:33:34.900
These ice formations are created afresh each winter as the long freeze sets in.

00:33:34.900 --> 00:33:38.600
This is Abisko National Park in the far north of Sweden.

00:33:38.600 --> 00:33:47.640
Its classic U-shaped glacial valley is called Lapporten, which means gateway to Lapland.

00:33:47.640 --> 00:33:55.480
On winter nights, it's the setting for a fleeting natural wonder which can sometimes be viewed

00:33:55.480 --> 00:34:00.280
across the entire Arctic.

00:34:00.280 --> 00:34:06.680
The small town of Abisko can be reached by rail, making it accessible to enthusiastic

00:34:06.680 --> 00:34:09.000
watchers of the night sky.

00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:38.920
Here, if you're lucky, you can observe one of nature's light shows, the Aurora Borealis.

00:34:38.920 --> 00:34:48.920
At the end of the long Arctic winter, summer comes suddenly to Abisko.

00:34:48.920 --> 00:35:08.760
The waterfalls and rivers, which for eight months were frozen solid, thaw to torrents.

00:35:08.760 --> 00:35:13.520
Northwards across the lake, the night sky retains an uncanny glow.

00:35:13.520 --> 00:35:30.360
Up here, the summer is short, but its hours of daylight are very long.

00:35:30.360 --> 00:35:38.320
For about a month, the sun never sets over Lapporten, but skims along the horizon.

00:35:38.320 --> 00:35:44.800
Here, in the land of the midnight sun, this annual event offers yet another natural night

00:35:44.800 --> 00:35:54.680
show.

00:35:54.680 --> 00:36:00.200
South to the world's second-largest continent, home to the only surviving ancient wonder

00:36:00.200 --> 00:36:06.000
of the world, the Egyptian pyramids.

00:36:06.000 --> 00:36:10.800
Despite its long human history, Africa is sparsely populated.

00:36:10.800 --> 00:36:19.640
Much of it is still unmarked by humanity.

00:36:19.640 --> 00:36:28.440
Rising majestically above the plains of Tanzania, Kilimanjaro is Africa's highest mountain.

00:36:28.440 --> 00:36:39.560
It was formed from three extinct volcanoes, which first erupted only a million years ago.

00:36:39.560 --> 00:36:45.600
Mount Kilimanjaro is just one of about 20 volcanoes rising from the bed of the Great

00:36:45.600 --> 00:36:47.320
Rift Valley.

00:36:47.320 --> 00:36:51.560
Mount Kenya is the second-highest mountain in Africa.

00:36:51.560 --> 00:36:55.740
Although it stands almost directly on the equator, there are permanent snowfields and

00:36:55.740 --> 00:36:58.200
glaciers around its summit.

00:36:58.200 --> 00:37:02.440
When it first formed, between two and a half and three million years ago, it was probably

00:37:02.440 --> 00:37:06.040
about 2,000 feet higher than it is today.

00:37:06.040 --> 00:37:13.600
And it had a volcanic crater at its summit, like Kilimanjaro.

00:37:13.600 --> 00:37:20.160
To the north, the world's longest river, the Nile, and its biggest desert, the Sahara.

00:37:20.160 --> 00:37:26.480
To the south, the wide plains of the Serengeti, famous for their abundant wildlife and studied

00:37:26.480 --> 00:37:28.400
with geological wonders.

00:37:28.400 --> 00:37:36.480
All evidence of the long and eventful history of this spectacular continent.

00:37:36.480 --> 00:37:49.320
Ngorongoro Crater, a gigantic basin 12 miles wide, is all that remains of an extinct volcano

00:37:49.320 --> 00:37:56.680
which once towered high above its present rim.

00:37:56.680 --> 00:38:03.640
The rift valley is a 4,000-mile-long fracture in the Earth's crust.

00:38:03.640 --> 00:38:07.280
Along its course, a series of lakes have formed.

00:38:07.280 --> 00:38:12.880
This is the Soda Lake, called Natron, and in the background rises one of Africa's active

00:38:12.880 --> 00:38:22.160
volcanoes, Mount Lengai.

00:38:22.160 --> 00:38:30.120
Lengai erupts every seven years or so, spewing out a black lava from its core.

00:38:30.120 --> 00:38:34.920
Within hours, this cools to a white powder, giving this steep-sided mountain a unique

00:38:34.920 --> 00:38:45.720
appearance that sets it apart from any other in the world.

00:38:45.720 --> 00:39:05.520
A thousand miles south of the equator, beyond the great rift, is Africa's most famous scenic

00:39:05.520 --> 00:39:09.240
wonder.

00:39:09.240 --> 00:39:16.200
There the wide, almost placid Zambezi River suddenly plunges over a 350-foot-high cliff,

00:39:16.200 --> 00:39:24.080
creating a fall that the Africans call Moseo Atunia, the smoke that thunders.

00:39:24.080 --> 00:39:29.280
When 19th-century explorer David Livingston became the first European to see these impressive

00:39:29.280 --> 00:39:36.240
falls, he named them in honor of his queen, Victoria.

00:39:36.240 --> 00:39:41.600
At the peak of the rains in March, so much water leaps into the chasm that the view becomes

00:39:41.600 --> 00:39:54.760
hidden by mist and spray.

00:39:54.760 --> 00:39:59.000
Our final great African wonder is the Namib Desert.

00:39:59.000 --> 00:40:08.000
It's not as large as the Sahara, but it's probably the world's oldest desert.

00:40:08.000 --> 00:40:13.760
Stretching for 1,300 miles, it fringes the treacherous Skeleton Coast of Namibia in the

00:40:13.760 --> 00:40:16.760
southwest of the continent.

00:40:16.760 --> 00:40:21.400
Here the prevailing westerly winds blowing in from the Atlantic drive the massive sand

00:40:21.400 --> 00:40:35.240
dunes far inland.

00:40:35.240 --> 00:40:40.720
The same westerly winds now carry visitors into this remote desert to experience the

00:40:40.720 --> 00:40:55.720
magnitude and splendor of this technicolor landscape with its ever-shifting shapes and

00:40:55.720 --> 00:41:01.320
colors.

00:41:01.320 --> 00:41:12.080
Statistically, this is the driest desert in Africa, but dense night fogs can roll in

00:41:12.080 --> 00:41:27.680
for 50 miles or more.

00:41:27.680 --> 00:41:33.120
The Namib is certainly a treasure in the literal sense of the word, for it's the richest source

00:41:33.120 --> 00:41:34.920
of diamonds on the planet.

00:41:34.920 --> 00:41:58.800
But for the traveler, its great glory lies in the stark beauty of its dunes.

00:41:58.800 --> 00:42:20.240
Heading due east from southern Africa, we cross the huge island of Madagascar and travel

00:42:20.240 --> 00:42:26.560
over the Indian Ocean for a further 4,000 miles before meeting a new dawn breaking over

00:42:26.560 --> 00:42:41.800
a very old continent.

00:42:41.800 --> 00:42:46.880
The familiar silhouettes of Sydney Harbor Bridge and the Opera House are the icons of

00:42:46.880 --> 00:42:55.800
this young nation.

00:42:55.800 --> 00:43:01.480
Just under 2,000 miles inland, almost at the very center of Australia, stand landmarks

00:43:01.480 --> 00:43:06.640
that have been revered for tens of thousands of years by the first people to arrive in

00:43:06.640 --> 00:43:10.800
this ancient land.

00:43:10.800 --> 00:43:16.720
Australia is the only nation to occupy an entire continent.

00:43:16.720 --> 00:43:24.840
Its mainland is the biggest island in the world, and its rocks are among the oldest.

00:43:24.840 --> 00:43:29.080
These beneath my feet are 3 billion years old.

00:43:29.080 --> 00:43:33.920
In that unimaginable time, they've been worn down by the wind and the rain and the

00:43:33.920 --> 00:43:38.120
baking sun and all the other processes of natural erosion.

00:43:38.120 --> 00:43:45.980
Until now, Australia is also the flattest continent on the planet.

00:43:45.980 --> 00:43:52.320
On a clear day, you have an uninterrupted view from the Olgas across 20 miles of desert

00:43:52.320 --> 00:43:58.480
to the great monolith that Aborigines call Uluru, and European travelers in the 19th

00:43:58.480 --> 00:44:04.960
century named Ayers Rock.

00:44:04.960 --> 00:44:15.120
It was originally formed by the upending of layers of sandstone still visible today.

00:44:15.120 --> 00:44:22.120
Erosion by wind and water has molded its gullies and created its smooth profile.

00:44:22.120 --> 00:44:27.880
Uluru catches what little rain falls on this otherwise arid landscape, creating a kind

00:44:27.880 --> 00:44:37.960
of oasis at its base.

00:44:37.960 --> 00:44:44.880
As light varies throughout the day, Uluru seems almost magically to change color.

00:44:44.880 --> 00:44:49.200
But this immense boulder is exceptional.

00:44:49.200 --> 00:44:58.160
For the most part, the Australian center is as flat a country as you can find anywhere.

00:44:58.160 --> 00:45:01.640
It's also one of the driest places on earth.

00:45:01.640 --> 00:45:06.160
Vast areas of it are desert, far from the influence of the sea.

00:45:06.160 --> 00:45:09.400
But there are 20,000 miles of coastline.

00:45:09.400 --> 00:45:19.760
And that is where most Australians live.

00:45:19.760 --> 00:45:26.600
Waves from the Antarctic beat against Australia's southern shores.

00:45:26.600 --> 00:45:32.560
Year by year, century by century, this coastline is retreating, leaving behind relics of its

00:45:32.560 --> 00:45:38.680
past frontiers.

00:45:38.680 --> 00:45:44.600
At the end of Melbourne, limestone stacks called the Twelve Apostles rise 150 feet above

00:45:44.600 --> 00:46:04.680
the waves.

00:46:04.680 --> 00:46:22.120
New stacks are constantly being formed as waves cut into the headlands on both sides.

00:46:22.120 --> 00:46:34.000
Harder rocks, more resistant to erosion, survive longer.

00:46:34.000 --> 00:46:39.420
The different layers of limestone were laid down as sediment on the floor of an ancient

00:46:39.420 --> 00:46:42.480
and long-vanished sea.

00:46:42.480 --> 00:47:04.840
The ocean is now reclaiming the land that it once helped to create.

00:47:04.840 --> 00:47:10.200
In the northwest of Australia is the remote and fascinating region of the Kimberleys.

00:47:10.200 --> 00:47:17.640
It has a jagged coastline with many inlets that reach deep inland.

00:47:17.640 --> 00:47:27.480
The tide sweeps into them with amazing speed and ferocity.

00:47:27.480 --> 00:47:31.400
Talbot Bay forms part of one of the larger inlets.

00:47:31.400 --> 00:47:36.320
From the air, you can see the way this almost landlocked bay is linked to the rest of the

00:47:36.320 --> 00:47:44.880
inlet by two narrow channels through which the tides race in and out.

00:47:44.880 --> 00:47:50.200
The inland exit of the bay narrows to 20 feet at water level, funneling the tide as it ebbs

00:47:50.200 --> 00:47:51.200
and flows.

00:47:51.200 --> 00:48:00.440
It's this twice-daily event that makes Talbot Bay Australia's most unusual natural wonder.

00:48:00.440 --> 00:48:03.120
The surest way in is by seaplane.

00:48:03.120 --> 00:48:06.840
Boats rarely venture here and there are no roads.

00:48:06.840 --> 00:48:11.800
Few people have ever seen this spectacle.

00:48:11.800 --> 00:48:17.880
As the tide floods in, millions of gallons surge into the bay, forcing the water onwards

00:48:17.880 --> 00:48:22.200
through the furthest narrows.

00:48:22.200 --> 00:48:28.580
The water level in the bay can rise by 36 feet, making it one of the largest tidal changes

00:48:28.580 --> 00:48:34.600
in the world.

00:48:34.600 --> 00:48:41.000
But it's the power and speed of the ebb and flow that is so impressive.

00:48:41.000 --> 00:48:46.120
The powerhouse that drives this phenomenon is to be found 30 miles out to sea on the

00:48:46.120 --> 00:48:53.200
huge Montgomery Reef that fringes this coastline.

00:48:53.200 --> 00:48:58.880
Here as the tide retreats from the vast shallow shelf, tremendous suction is generated as

00:48:58.880 --> 00:49:05.500
the ocean pours off the reef.

00:49:05.500 --> 00:49:09.560
It takes four hours for the effect to reach Talbot Bay.

00:49:09.560 --> 00:49:35.280
Then the tide turns and the water flows back towards the sea.

00:49:35.280 --> 00:49:50.200
Known as the horizontals, these extraordinary tidal races through Talbot Bay are a compelling

00:49:50.200 --> 00:50:05.920
reminder of the colossal power of the ocean tides.

00:50:05.920 --> 00:50:11.920
Here in the arid heart of the continent, it's hot and dry for most of the time, hardly the

00:50:11.920 --> 00:50:14.960
place for large human settlements.

00:50:14.960 --> 00:50:21.000
But that's what makes the outback for me so unforgettable and so important.

00:50:21.000 --> 00:50:26.840
Here you can sense the great antiquity of the earth and the trivial length of time that

00:50:26.840 --> 00:50:45.640
humanity has been part of its history.

00:50:45.640 --> 00:50:53.420
The Poonorulu National Park is also part of the Kimberley region of northwestern Australia.

00:50:53.420 --> 00:50:59.760
This puzzling landscape is known as the Bungle Bungles.

00:50:59.760 --> 00:51:05.640
The bedrock of this plateau is very ancient, laid down in the estuaries of rivers early

00:51:05.640 --> 00:51:09.240
in the earth's history.

00:51:09.240 --> 00:51:16.520
But the curious beehive-shaped domes of sandstone are comparatively recent creations, shaped

00:51:16.520 --> 00:51:20.520
during the last 20 million years.

00:51:20.520 --> 00:51:26.600
The bizarre striping is due to the way alternate layers of sandstone have developed a protective

00:51:26.600 --> 00:51:32.480
coating of either red iron oxide or blue-green lichens.

00:51:32.480 --> 00:51:36.880
If it were not for this natural covering, the fragile sandstone shapes would have been

00:51:36.880 --> 00:51:45.740
worn down long ago.

00:51:45.740 --> 00:51:52.200
This jewel of the Kimberleys is now one of Australia's 500 national parks, all illustrating

00:51:52.200 --> 00:52:09.600
chapters in the country's long and dramatic geological story.

00:52:09.600 --> 00:52:14.120
The largest of Australia's natural wonders is the Great Barrier Reef.

00:52:14.120 --> 00:52:19.960
It fringes 1,400 miles of the tropical Queensland coast.

00:52:19.960 --> 00:52:26.140
It's a complex mosaic of 3,000 individual reefs and many small islands, all built of

00:52:26.140 --> 00:52:29.240
coral and sand.

00:52:29.240 --> 00:52:48.920
Evolved over hundreds of thousands of years, it's one of nature's most impressive creations.

00:52:48.920 --> 00:52:53.840
The first astronauts to circle the earth reported they could make out the colour and shapes

00:52:53.840 --> 00:53:16.120
of the individual reefs and lagoons and the vivid blue of the deeper, open water in between.

00:53:16.120 --> 00:53:21.280
Closer to the surface, the separate coral formations become discernible.

00:53:21.280 --> 00:53:27.400
Each one is a community of different kinds of coral that between them create these intricate

00:53:27.400 --> 00:53:35.760
limestone structures.

00:53:35.760 --> 00:53:42.800
This colony of brain coral is 20 feet in diameter and perhaps 2,000 years old, just one component

00:53:42.800 --> 00:53:59.880
part of the largest living structure on our planet.

00:53:59.880 --> 00:54:08.160
In contrast to Australia, New Zealand has a skyline dominated by mountains.

00:54:08.160 --> 00:54:17.000
Tallest of all is Mount Cook, part of the young, rugged spine of South Island.

00:54:17.000 --> 00:54:20.320
The southwest coast is cut through by fjords.

00:54:20.320 --> 00:54:26.640
This is Milford Sound, 12 miles long, 1,000 feet deep and flanked by the world's tallest

00:54:26.640 --> 00:54:33.040
sea cliffs.

00:54:33.040 --> 00:54:40.120
To the south of New Zealand, the Great Southern Ocean encircles the seventh continent, Antarctica,

00:54:40.120 --> 00:54:48.720
the most remote and inhospitable place on earth.

00:54:48.720 --> 00:54:54.520
Seventy percent of the planet's entire reserves of fresh water are locked up in Antarctica's

00:54:54.520 --> 00:55:00.720
massive ice cap.

00:55:00.720 --> 00:55:05.040
And near the fringes of the continent are their visible signs of mountains.

00:55:05.040 --> 00:55:18.280
The great bulk of the land is out of sight, hidden beneath two miles of permanent ice.

00:55:18.280 --> 00:55:22.880
Here at the bottom of the world is its greatest wilderness.

00:55:22.880 --> 00:55:27.560
Its bleakness is a reminder of how much of our planet must have looked during its many

00:55:27.560 --> 00:55:37.600
ice ages.

00:55:37.600 --> 00:55:42.800
In the summer months, the margins of the continent warm to the long hours of sunlight.

00:55:42.800 --> 00:55:53.720
Antarctica becomes a little less hostile.

00:55:53.720 --> 00:56:06.840
The sea thaws and the first summer visitors arrive.

00:56:06.840 --> 00:56:12.960
Humpback whales are worldwide travelers, breeding in the tropics and migrating to the two polar

00:56:12.960 --> 00:56:17.880
regions to feed on the summer bonanza of krill.

00:56:17.880 --> 00:56:24.840
They enjoy the best of both worlds.

00:56:24.840 --> 00:56:30.600
Throughout our world, there's a rich diversity of life.

00:56:30.600 --> 00:56:36.560
That in itself is a direct consequence of the wonderfully varied natural features and

00:56:36.560 --> 00:56:41.240
landscapes of our planet.

00:56:41.240 --> 00:56:46.960
But no matter what the future of life may be, nothing will change the forces that shape

00:56:46.960 --> 00:56:50.280
the face of our planet long before life began.
